          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stendhal-le-rouge-et-le-noir-e77cc1cde2107c64\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"Amanda’s conditional “or I shall no longer love you” presents the cessation of her affection as the consequence of Julien’s refusing to leave. Its principal force is truth-conditional, though hypothetical and rhetorically coercive rather than a report of her present state. The threatened withdrawal of affection gives slight support to P, but it is not clearly a serious relational undertaking. Nothing indicates that the words themselves are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The threatened loss of love may be either a literal conditional prediction or an expedient means of compelling Julien to leave; this affects sincerity, not the primarily truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Amanda directly addresses Julien with a conditional cessative love utterance: “Sortez… ou je ne vous aime plus.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Amanda is frightened that Julien will provoke her other lover and urgently tries to remove him from danger. She uses threatened withdrawal of affection as leverage, immediately adding “et cependant, je vous aime bien,” which confirms present affection while softening the threat. Her agitation and strategic persuasion do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Sortez à l'instant du café, ou je ne vous aime plus” (“Leave the café this instant, or I shall no longer love you”).\",\"supports\":\"The disjunctive imperative frames the love clause as a conditional consequence and persuasive threat, strongly supporting T and weakly supporting P.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Amanda immediately continues: “et cependant, je vous aime bien.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present avowal indicates that “je ne vous aime plus” projects a future cessation rather than describing her current feelings.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Amanda is “aussi agitée que lui et fort rouge” and repeats the words quietly while slowly returning Julien’s change.\",\"supports\":\"Her delivery is urgent and emotionally charged, but the narration does not characterize the words as an involuntary or reflex-like discharge; this supports E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Amanda has seized Julien by the arm and is trying to prevent him from approaching the threatening man at the billiard table.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate practical aim is to make Julien leave safely, explaining the coercive or strategic use of the conditional love claim.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense, negative, cessative form with the modifier “no longer.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted core concerns conditional cessation of love rather than a simple positive avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the conditional presentation of affection’s cessation, while a low P score registers limited undertaking-like pressure from the threat. Strategic motivation and agitation are contextual and create no residual need for O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification concerns the highlighted negative clause “je ne vous aime plus,” interpreted within the full conditional sentence.\"}"
        }
      ],
